# Line-ending policy. Without this, core.autocrlf rewrites files on checkout and the
# bytes differ per platform — which silently breaks a hash pin.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all goes FIRST and the
# byte-frozen overrides come after it. (Putting `* text=auto` last silently defeats
# every -text rule below it.)

# Default: normalise to LF in the repo, let git do what it likes on checkout.
* text=auto

# THE LOAD-BEARING RULE: serving/app.py verify_manifest() refuses to start unless
# sha256(data/real/corpus_v3.csv) == the manifest's corpus.sha256. That hash is over
# the file's BYTES, so any CRLF<->LF conversion changes it and the Endpoint dies with
# CORPUS HASH MISMATCH. -text freezes the bytes: every clone, on every OS, checks out
# byte-identical to what was measured.
data/real/*.csv     -text
data/fixtures/*.csv -text

# Same reasoning for artifacts a manifest/report hash may cover.
reports/*.json      -text
reports/*.csv       -text

# Generated/vendored — keep them out of diffs and language stats.
*.html              -text linguist-generated
uv.lock             -diff
